Date: Sat, 29 May 93 13:25:13 CDT
From: ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu
Subject: Contrapositive Crows

Since Perry raised the subject of contrapositives, it seems like a good
excuse to post one of my favorite puzzle/paradoxes.

Claim: A sheet of white paper is evidence that all crows are black.

Proof:

1. If you observe an A and it is B, that is evidence for the proposition
"All A's are B." By "evidence for" I mean that after the experiment your
probability for the truth of the proposition is higher than before, unless
your prior probability for its being true was zero (i.e. you have already
seen an A that is not a B). Those to whom this is not obvious are invited
to apply the probabilistic argument that I posted recently in another
thread.

2. "All Crows are black" is equivalent to "If X is a crow then X is black."

3. "If X is a crow then X is black" is equivalent to "If X is not black,
then X is not a crow"--its contrapositive.

4.  "If X is not black, then X is not a crow" is equivalent to "All not
black things are not crows."

5. A sheet of white paper is a not black thing that is not a crow, hence by
1 above it is evidence for the proposition  "All not black things are not
crows." Hence by 2-4 it is evidence for the (equivalent) proposition "All
Crows are black."

David Friedman
University of Chicago Law School


