Subject: New Brain Test: "Einstein einstein.... Penrose!"

In the seventies, most of the papers on computer perception were a cloud of
squidlike ink, emitted to cover a panicky retreat.  They mostly had negative
signal to noise ratio, and were composed of misdirection, obfuscation,
complexification, misleading neologisms, and meaningless pseudo sentences. 
Fortunately most of these papers are now utterly forgotten.

Today's papers on perception by neural nets increasingly resemble the
computer perception papers of the seventies.  The neural net systems suffer
from the same weakness as conventional approaches did in the seventies, and
papers written about them obfuscate this weakness in the same way.

Papers on simulation of simple brains are showing mild early symptoms of this
disease, though they have not yet come down with the full blown and terminal
sickness.

In <9305282231.AA08207@wookumz.gnu.ai.mit.edu>, pcm@cs.brown.edu (Peter C. McCluskey)  wrote:

>  The term "non polynomially large number of steps" is meaningless without
> reference to a variable that you are scaling up. Whatever you have in mind,
> I doubt that anyone knows what the lower bound is.

We know with confidence that the lower bound of the scaling variable for
realistic and typical problems that simple animals effortlessly solve all
the time is at least several times larger than the lower bound for the
microworld problems that existing neural nets can do.  Existing neural nets
cannot handle micro worlds as large as those handled by conventional AI in
the seventies.

Best seventies microworld is:
T Kanade Artificial Intelligence 13 279-311 (80)
T Kanade Artificial Intelligence 17 409-460 (81) (negative chair 
experiment) (One of the few lucid papers of that era.)

Since the amount of processing power required goes up nonpolynomially fast
with the scaling variable, (in the case of "neural nets" the size of the
neural net") no realistic computer, now or in the distant future, can make a
noticeable difference, using existing methods.  Nobody has significantly
improved on early microworlds even though today's computers are enormously
faster than computers in those days.  Power just does not make much
difference against nonpolynomial growth. Our problem is more fundamental
than mere power.

> >Neural networks supposedly modeled on natural neural networks conspicuously
> >fail to display perceptual invariance, which is a major and obvious feature
> >of the capabilities of most simple animals, and the major design goal of
> >most neural network designs.
> 
>  If by perceptual invariance you mean the ability to recognize objects at
> independent of position, orientation, size, and color, then neural networks
> have been fairly successful. I refer you to:

(Long list of papers making claims about the power of neural networks
listed.)

Of those claims that I am familiar with (in so far as they relate to
actual accomplishments rather than vague promises) all yield the same 
fragile and brittle microworld perceptual invariance as standard 
AI approaches.)

Artificial "neural nets" fail to provide perceptual invariance in that the
required size of the neural net grows exponentially with each additional
source of variation, which is obviously not the case with real neural nets.

The point of my citation of Keith Kendrick
Keith M. Kendrick Phys Abs 7-133282, Science USA Vol. 236 no 4800 p448- 50
New Scientist 12 May 1990 No 1716 p62
Was that high level category recognition is close to feature extraction,
both in timing and in physical location on the visual pathway.  This is
contrary to what is necessary in artificial "neural nets" which falsely claim
to emulate what real animals do.  Such "neural nets" require many, many steps
between feature extraction and high level category recognition.

My guess is that the problem will be solved by new and unexpected discoveries
about the nature of information processing in individual cells.  A
fundamental scientific breakthrough is needed.  Engineering is
irrelevant to this problem.

